## Problem

In Chinese/Japanese/Korean mixed-language text, the `ARTICLES` regex `/\b(?:a|an|the)\s+(?=[a-z])/gi` incorrectly strips English articles that carry semantic weight:

| Original | After compress | Problem |
|----------|---------------|---------|
| "这是一个 **a** LLM 模型" | "这是一个 LLM 模型" | Lost "a" = loses "one/some" nuance |
| "这个 **the** MCP server" | "这个 MCP server" | Lost "the" = loses "this specific" nuance |

Chinese has no article concept — the English article is the **only** way to express specificity for embedded technical terms.

## Fix

Add CJK detection before `ARTICLES` removal in `compressProse()`. When CJK characters are present in the text, skip the `ARTICLES` regex entirely. All other compression (fillers, pleasantries, hedges, leaders) still applies — those are genuinely filler in any language context.

Also replaces NUL-byte sentinels (`\x00`) with Unicode PUA (`U+E000`) in `withProtectedSegments()`. The original code used NUL bytes as placeholder sentinels, which causes Git to misidentify `compress.js` as binary, making the diff invisible on GitHub. No functional change.

## Diff (GitHub shows binary due to NUL bytes in base)

