## Bug

In `index.js:106`, the guard condition `if (!compressedSomething)` prevents `compressDescriptionsInPlace()` from running on nested schemas when any top-level description was compressed:

```javascript
// Line 82-99: compresses top-level item.description fields
for (const field of fields) {
  if (typeof item[field] === 'string') {
    // ... compress, sets compressedSomething = true
  }
}

// Line 105-106: nested walk is SKIPPED because compressedSomething is true
if (!compressedSomething) compressDescriptionsInPlace(r, fields);
```

## Impact

MCP tool definitions have two layers of descriptions:
- Top-level `description`: 9% of total JSON (e.g., 5,386 chars for mcp-atlassian's 44 tools)
- `inputSchema.properties.*.description`: **33.7%** of total JSON (20,155 chars)

Since most MCP servers have top-level descriptions, `compressedSomething` is always `true`, and the `compressDescriptionsInPlace` recursive walk never runs. This means **77% of compressible text is untouched**.

## Measured data

Tested against `mcp-atlassian` (44 tools, 59,836 chars total JSON):

| Version | Top-level desc | Param desc | Total saved |
|---|---|---|---|
| Current (v0.1.0) | 5,386 -> 5,153 (4.3%) | 20,155 -> 20,155 (0%) | 233 chars |
| With fix | 5,386 -> 5,153 (4.3%) | 20,155 -> 19,322 (4.1%) | 1,066 chars |

## Suggested fix

Replace line 106 with targeted nested walk that avoids double-processing top-level descriptions:

```javascript
for (const arrayName of ['tools', 'prompts', 'resources', 'resourceTemplates']) {
  if (Array.isArray(r[arrayName])) {
    for (const item of r[arrayName]) {
      if (item.inputSchema) compressDescriptionsInPlace(item.inputSchema, fields);
    }
  }
}
```

`compress()` is idempotent, so even if a description were processed twice, the second pass returns the same string.

### Summary

`src/hooks/caveman-activate.js` appends a `STATUSLINE SETUP NEEDED` block to its SessionStart output whenever `settings.json` has no `statusLine` key, including the instruction "Proactively offer to set this up for the user on first interaction." There is no way to decline it permanently.

### Current behavior

```js
let hasStatusline = false;
if (fs.existsSync(settingsPath)) {
  const settings = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(settingsPath, 'utf8'));
  if (settings.statusLine) {
    hasStatusline = true;
  }
}

if (!hasStatusline) {
  output += "\n\n" + "STATUSLINE SETUP NEEDED: ... Proactively offer to set this up for the user on first interaction.";
}
```

The check is presence-only. The only way to silence it is to define some `statusLine` in settings, which is exactly what a user who does not want a statusline is trying to avoid. Because SessionStart stdout is injected into the model's context, the nudge reappears every single session and the assistant offers the badge again each time.

### Why the obvious workaround does not work

Defining a no-op statusline to satisfy the presence check looks like a clean out:

```json
"statusLine": { "type": "command", "command": "true" }
```

It is not. Claude Code renders the statusline as roughly `text ? <StatusText/> : <Box>" "</Box>`, so a command that outputs nothing still draws a blank row, and that row displaces default footer information. Verified on Claude Code 2.1.238. So users who want no statusline at all are stuck between an unwanted blank row and a recurring nudge.

### Request

An explicit opt-out. Any of these would do, in rough order of preference:

1. An environment variable, for example `CAVEMAN_STATUSLINE_NUDGE=0`, checked alongside the existing `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` lookup.
2. A key in the plugin's own state file, so `/caveman` could expose something like `/caveman no-badge`.
3. Suppress the nudge permanently after it has been shown once, rather than every session.

Option 1 is a two line change and would cover scripted and dotfile-managed setups.

### Workaround in use

Rewriting `let hasStatusline = false;` to `true` in the cached plugin file. Plugin cache directories are version hashed, so this has to be reapplied by a SessionStart hook after every plugin update.

