- caveman plugin from the `JuliusBrussee/caveman` marketplace

`clearSessionPrev` in `src/hooks/caveman-config.js` deletes both the per-session and legacy prev-mode files with a bare `fs.unlinkSync`:

```js
function clearSessionPrev(claudeDir, sessionId) {
  const p = sessionPrevPath(claudeDir, sessionId);
  if (p) {
    try { fs.unlinkSync(p); } catch (e) {}
    return;
  }
  try { fs.unlinkSync(path.join(claudeDir, PREV_BASENAME)); } catch (e) {}
}
```

`safeWriteFlag` refuses to write a flag through a parent symlink owned by another user, but a bare `unlinkSync` follows that same symlink without any check, so a file the write path would never have created there can still be deleted through it.

#1055 fixed the identical asymmetry for `writeSessionMode`'s legacy-mirror delete by routing it through `safeDeleteFlag`, which is already exported from this file. `clearSessionPrev`'s two delete sites never got the same treatment.

Practical impact is limited: nothing currently reads either prev file back as a trust signal, so this is the same class of hardening #1055 already covered, not a new attack in itself.

